International analysis: Will Russian oil pricing end the Ukraine war?
MOSCOW (Reuters) - Russia's maritime oil prices agreed by the European Union, the G7 and Australia came into force on Monday, December 5, 2022. The $ 60-a-barrel price tag was aimed at reducing Russia's revenue from its use or supply for its war of aggression in Ukraine.
The Ukrainian president added that the international community had shown weakness by setting the price of Russian oil at $ 60 a barrel, and his top adviser said the price of Russian oil should be set at $ 30. So whether the $ 60 price tag could cut Russia's economic revenue or end its aggression in Ukraine? How will this oil pricing work?
