Sion L. Morton, ADB Senior Economist, said: "The Kovid 19 pandemic has had a devastating effect on key sectors of the Cambodian economy, such as garments, footwear and textiles, as well as tourism and construction. "Reforms under this program will help Cambodia's post-epidemic recovery goals by paving the way for businesses to grow and reach higher value-added segments and adapt to the changing business landscape."
Business programs and competitiveness will help improve the overall business environment for local and foreign companies. In connection with this initial sub-program, the Royal Government has approved a new investment law to provide legal protection for investors, including investors in special economic zones, and upgraded its online business portal to enable the issuance of specialized digital business licenses. Time.
The program supports the development and diversification of MSMEs, improving access to finance and creating new markets for them. It includes streamlining the legal definition of small and medium enterprises and launching a government-funded grant package to provide technical assistance and assistance to small and medium enterprises in priority industries with greater women's participation.
The program will also help Cambodia improve the implementation of regional trade agreements and strengthen trade facilitation work to provide more opportunities for Cambodian businesses to export their goods. In connection with the reforms under the first sub-program, the Royal Government has established the National Committee for Trade Facilitation and approved the National Roadmap on Trade Facilitation.
