Japan provides over 180,000 USD for bridge construction and irrigation rehabilitation in Kampong Speu and Kampong Cham
(Phnom Penh): The Government of Japan through the Embassy of Cambodia has provided a total of $ 186,856 to two recipients under the Grant Program for the Small-Scale Human Security Project (Kusanoni Grant).
The grant agreement was signed on November 5, 2022 by Mr. Taninai Ichitomo, Ambassador of the Embassy of Japan to Cambodia, Mr. Su Sunthara, Director of the Department of Public Works and Transport of Kampong Speu Province, and Mr. Um Vibol, Director of the Department of Water Resources and Environment. Meteorology of Kampong Cham Province.
* First: Bridge construction project in Omlaing commune, Thpong district, Kampong Speu province (budget $ 99,212) Recipient: Department of Public Works and Transport, Kampong Speu province.
* Second: Tuol Andet Irrigation Rehabilitation Project in Kampong Cham Province ($ 87,644) Recipient: Department of Water Resources and Meteorology of Kampong Cham Province.
The grant will be used to rehabilitate three damaged dams and culverts in Prey Chhor district. These damaged irrigation facilities do not store enough rainwater during the rainy season. As a result, the water supply for daily use is insufficient and farming is unstable depending on rainfall.
The project aims to reduce daily water shortages during the dry season, and we expect the project to benefit more than 4,500 people. The grant will also contribute to increasing farmers' incomes by ensuring a stable water supply for agriculture.
According to Tanai Ichitumo, since 1991, in addition to the large-scale credit and grant projects, the Japanese government has been implementing the Kusanoni program in Cambodia to directly assist local authorities and NGOs in Implement their project. Kusanoni means "grass root" in Japanese.
"In the last 30 years, the Embassy of Japan in Cambodia has contributed to more than 660 projects across Cambodia through the Kusanoni Grant. We are pleased that these projects have directly contributed to improving the quality of life of Cambodians at the grassroots level. ”
At the same time, the Ambassador of the Embassy of Japan expressed his pleasure to observe that Cambodia has been developing rapidly recently. However, he acknowledged that there are still some challenges that need to be addressed to improve people's quality of life. Japan will continue to contribute to addressing these challenges through various economic and development cooperation schemes, including "Kusanoni".
