BEIJING (Reuters) - Risks to the stability of China's financial system are on the rise as the country's real estate sector slows and the economy slows, making smaller banks smaller, the credit rating agency Moody's said on Friday. Vulnerable. According to Reuters.
China's real estate sector has shrunk sharply this year after Beijing imposed measures to curb over-borrowing from developers. The effort has led to a slump in real estate investment, sales and prices, and a rise in bond failures.
"Some financial system bonds are falling, which could pose a risk if the real estate slump continues," Reuters reported, citing a report released by Moody's. The declining demand has maintained a negative outlook for the real estate sector, it added.
"Risks to the stability of China's financial system are increasing amid a slowdown in China's real estate sector and economic recovery," the report said. In recent weeks, Beijing has stepped up its support for increasing liquidity in the industry, which accounts for a quarter of China's economy, the world's second-largest economy and a major driver of economic growth.
The country's major commercial banks have also provided at least $ 162 billion in new loans to real estate developers.
While the new Chinese government policy may ease fiscal constraints, it will take time to achieve results, the report said.
According to Moody's, although the banking system is generally robust, small banks are extremely vulnerable and face many risks from the real estate sector. According to Reuters.
