Samdech Techo Hun Sen Drops $ 30 Million in National Mine Action Plan and Calls on Donors to Support Mine Action
(Phnom Penh): Samdech Techo Hun Sen, the Prime Minister of Cambodia, emphasized that Samdech has just decided to reduce the national budget of about 30 million US dollars to the Mine and Unexploded Ordnance Disposal Fund, which is controlled by the Ministry of Economy and Finance. This budget, together with funds from development partners, can continue to be used in 2023.
On the Facebook page on the evening of Thursday, December 1, 2022, Samdech Techo Prime Minister highlighted that the total budget of 18,473,570 dollars, which is a royal gift from the King and Queen Mother and philanthropists for demining. It will be paid by the morning of December 1, 2022.
Samdech Techo underlined that in order to ensure that Cambodia will run out of landmines by 2025, Samdech discussed with Deputy Prime Minister Aun Porn Moniroth, Minister of Economy and Finance, to release the Royal Government's $ 30 million savings fund to the Mine Action Fund. And unexploded ordnance controlled by the Ministry of Economy and Finance.
The Prime Minister added that in case of shortage and for 2024 and 2025, the Mine Authority must work with the Ministry of Economy and Finance to prepare an annual budget.
With the confirmation of the reduction of the national budget in the demining program, Samdech Techo Hun Sen also continued to call on philanthropists to participate as much as possible so that in 2025, Cambodia will be mine-free, provide land safely and with a smile to the people.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also dedicated his prayers for 2025 to be a year without deaths, injuries and a year of smiles after 55 years of landmines (1970-2025).
It should be noted that due to the war torn apart since the late 1960s, Cambodia has been turned into a minefield and buried millions of unexploded ordnance. After overthrowing the genocidal regime of Pol Pot and finding strong internal unity with the help of friendly countries, Cambodia began clearing mines and unexploded ordnance left over from the war in 1992. By 2022, it will be 30 years.
