Three major union leaders express dissatisfaction with Central Organization's actions in abusing union work
(Phnom Penh): Three major union leaders have expressed dissatisfaction with the activities of the Central Organization of Mr. Moeun Tola, which violated the work of the union by attracting union members and providing funds to support separatist unions to form new unions.
Pav Sina, president of the Coalition of Workers' Movement Unions (CUMW), observed that Central was now acting as a union, as it was a civil society organization registered with the Ministry of Interior that could only act in accordance with the law. However, Central went to the union activities in violation of the registered legal principles.
He claimed that Central had lured his members at a factory in Banteay Meanchey province to secede and formed a new union, which he called for an end to such activities and asked the Ministry of Interior to monitor its activities.
Mr. Fasali, President of the National Confederation of Trade Unions, considered Central to be deeply involved in the union profession as well as to form unions that are not Central's work. He said Central had really interfered, including financing at some factories.
However, he did not sue the Central Organization, but called on the organization to immediately stop the above activities and asked the Ministry to monitor the activities that create any movement that is unprofessional and illegal.
At the same time, Mr. Kong Athit, President of CCAWDU, also expressed dissatisfaction with the activities of Central. He believes that between organizations and unions, there should be a clear division of labor and responsibilities related to social work. But the decision is an internal matter of this organization. He said that if the organization continues to abuse the work of the union, it will certainly create conflict. However, he did not consider the central action to be political.
The unions and federations claim that CENTRAL officials have been secretly working to incite their local union representatives who have attended CENTRAL training programs and seminars to form a union. New by promising to provide both technical and financial support.
WASHINGTON: The administration of US President Joe Biden has approved a long-standing arms deal with Qatar, agreeing to supply the Middle East ally with $ 1 billion worth of UAVs. This is according to the publication by the Russian newspaper RT on Wednesday, November 30, 2022.
Qatar has been bidding for UAVs, including MQ-9 drones, by 2020. But the US State Department just announced its approval Tuesday, confirming that the deal includes the sale of 10 state-of-the-art drone systems, 200 drone interceptors and other related equipment.
According to the State Department, the sale of UAVs to Qatar is part of US foreign policy and national security, aimed at improving the security of friendly countries, which continues to be a major force for political stability and progress. Economy in the Middle East. It will also improve Qatar's ability to withstand current and future threats by providing anti-drone capabilities.
Although relations between Doha and Washington are close, US officials have been slow to ratify the agreement due to concerns over its relations with other countries in the region, including Saudi Arabia and the United States. Also the United Arab Emirates. Saudi Arabia, the United Arab Emirates, Bahrain and Egypt severed ties with Qatar in 2017, accusing the country of supporting terrorists.
