South Korea moves fighter jets after Chinese, Russian military planes fly into air defense zone
SEOUL (Reuters) - The South Korean military said on Wednesday it had moved its fighter jets after two Chinese military planes and six Russian military planes flew into a designated area for protection. Air or South Korea's ADIZ. This was released by the AFP news agency on Wednesday, November 30, 2022.
According to Seoul, Chinese and Russian military aircraft are likely to be in joint air drills, but South Korean military aircraft, including F-15K fighter jets, have been mobilized to prepare for the situation. Any emergency occurs. Chinese and Russian military aircraft did not enter South Korean airspace directly.
The ADIZ extends beyond the sovereign airspace of each country and is designed to request that foreign aircraft identify themselves to avoid any unintentional collisions.
