SINGAPORE (Reuters) - Singapore lifted the ban on homosexuals but retained the legal definition of marriage (between men and women) after parliament approved changes to two draft laws on Tuesday. This is according to the release by CNA on Tuesday, November 29, 2022.
The repeal of Article 377A of the colonial period in the Penal Code was approved by a 93-3 majority majority. Amendments to the constitution that maintain the legal definition of marriage were approved by a majority of 85 to 2.
In Singapore, attitudes toward homosexuality have shifted toward greater liberalism in recent years, especially among young people, although conservative attitudes remain among religious groups.
Among young people between the ages of 18 and 25, about 42 percent accepted same-sex marriage in 2018, up from 17 percent five years ago, according to a survey by the Institute for Policy Studies.
