The order also states that the parties who do not agree with this order may file an appeal (requesting the revocation of the protection order) in accordance with the provisions of Article 550 and Article 557 of the Code of Civil Procedure.
The Phnom Penh Municipal Court on the afternoon of October 7, 2022, announced the verdicts in the two public defamation cases of Mr. Son Chhay, which were filed by the National Election Committee and the Cambodian People's Party.
In the case filed by the NEC, the judge sentenced Mr. Son Chhay to a fine of 9 million riels. In the case filed by the Cambodian People's Party, the judge sentenced Son Chhay to a fine of 8 million riels and ordered the civil party to pay 3,000 million riels in damages.
The NEC and the CPP filed the complaint after Son Chhay alleged that the 2022 5th Mandate Commune / Sangkat Council Election had resulted in vote-buying and fraud.
(Phnom Penh): Residents volunteering to move out of the Angkor resort area and the area controlled by the APSARA Authority have started to build houses in the Run Ta Ek development area after the authorities have cleared the forest and filled the land on their plots. .
Residents volunteering to move out of the Angkor resort area and the area managed by the APSARA Authority were given a plot of land measuring 20 meters by 30 meters in the Run Ta Ek Development Zone in Run Ta Ek Commune, Banteay Srei District, Siem Reap Province, more than 20 kilometers away. From Siem Reap.
After the clearing and clearing of forest land, housing and infrastructure by the Samdech Techo Engineering Corps of Siem Reap Province led by General Kheng Someth, many plots of land have been cleared and filled.
Along with the preparation of residential land, the team also opened for people to build houses. Many families are rushing to build their homes in the Run Ta Ek development area. The houses are built according to the possibilities of each family. Some families can afford to build stone houses, less able families can also build wooden houses with zinc provided by the Royal Government.
So Pov, a volunteer from Angkor, said her family had begun building toilets and fencing and would begin dismantling their homes in December.
Ms. So Pov noted that the relevant authorities have made efforts to prepare residential land and build infrastructure for people to live better. Ms. So Pov emphasized that "they really do, not leave the people."
Another resident who is coming to build a house in the Run Ta Ek development area also expressed his satisfaction with the infrastructure provided to support the people's livelihood.
Ms. Khay Khuon pointed out that the authorities have done a lot to prepare the land, housing and infrastructure.
Both So Pov and Khay Khuon expressed their satisfaction with coming to live in Run Ta Ek. In particular, owning land and being able to do whatever you want on that land, unlike in the Angkor resort, can not do anything.
Please note that the people who need to be prepared to live in this development area are more than 5,000 families. Samdech Techo Hun Sen instructed to clear the forest, prepare the land and have the force to help transport when dismantling the house from the Angkor resort.
In addition, Samdech Techo has organized the construction of infrastructure, roads, electricity, water supply, telephone antennas, markets, schools, health centers and pagodas to support the lives of people in the Run Ta Ek area.
Samdech Techo Hun Sen also awarded a Level 1 Poverty Equity Card and a 10-year urban rate bonus to people living in the area. This is to alleviate their monthly difficulties.
