Wildlife research officers from the Ministry of Environment and the research team received training on technical methods to equip elephants to monitor their movements.
(Phnom Penh): Elephants are the largest terrestrial mammals, intelligent and live in herds. Similar to human behavior, elephants need to live in private spaces, which can make it difficult to understand their movements in large natural forests, such as in the eastern landscape of Mondulkiri province.
Mr. Neth Pheaktra, Secretary of State and spokesman for the Ministry of Environment, welcomed the move, stating that this is a contribution to increase the knowledge and strengthen the capacity of Cambodian officials to better understand the techniques and methods of elephant conservation.
The Secretary of State for the Ministry of Environment claims: “According to the data from the automatic cameras in the protected areas, the Asian elephants live in herds in the protected areas of Cambodia and have many more young children. "The presence of newborn baby elephants in large herds is the hope of conservationists and environmentalists."
According to expert research, in the last 60 to 70 years, the number of Asian elephants in the world has decreased by about 50%. Many of its habitats have also been lost. Of the total number of Asian elephants, 39,463 to 47,427 are living in the wild, with about 75% living in India and Sri Lanka.
First: Reduce habitat loss Second: Preserve and reconnect corridors and herds of wild elephants Third: Strengthen the implementation of the law 4: Prevent the capture of wild elephants for adoption Between wild elephants and humans; and 6: Educate, disseminate and launch strategic actions to address those issues.
