Thai government urges tour operators to focus on spending rather than tourists
(Bangkok): Thailand's Ministry of Natural Resources and Environment has urged its tourism industry to focus on per capita spending rather than total number of tourists to help preserve the environment, recommending the implementation of policies such as restrictions. Number of visitors to tourist sites and so on.
The Bangkok Post reports that the Thai Ministry of Natural Resources and Environment has already put in place a capacity-limiting policy to limit the number of tourists and the length of stay in some of Thailand's national parks.
For example, the famous Maya Beach in Krabi allows only 380 visitors a day to provide the best experience for tourists and preserve the natural resources of the site. This policy is based on various studies.
He said the implementation of the policy would not affect tourism as more people would want to visit well-maintained destinations.
Other measures include the use of instant online booking form to purchase single-use plastic ban tickets in various areas and the development of integrated security infrastructure for tourists. According to the Bangkok Post.
