The National Bank of Cambodia organizes the 9th Macroeconomic Conference to find additional ways to recover Cambodia's economy after the 19 crisis
This topic will continue to be discussed to find the best way to recover Cambodia's economy after the Kovid 19 crisis.
Ms. Chea Serey said that the Macroeconomic Conference aims to strengthen and build a culture of research in Cambodia and provide a forum for discussions between researchers, policy makers, experts and stakeholders on key topics related to And the sustainable and sustainable development of the Cambodian economy.
She added that currently the outbreak of Kovid 19 in Cambodia is very small and there is nothing to worry about, which has allowed economic activity in key sectors to continue to recover gradually. In this regard, economic recovery plans and related policies should be studied and discussed in depth.
She said that in order to gather input for consideration in launching more new policies in the Cambodian economy, it is continuing to grow and continue to sow a wider research culture. "Journey to Recovery and Resilience after Kovid-19" for 2022.
In addition, Ms. Chea Serey shared some comments as additional input for the discussion, including:
1: Digitalization: In the past, it has been observed that the way of life in society and the work in institutions have changed drastically, shifting to the use of online more, whether in business, work or education that will bring Many benefits to the national economy by increasing productivity and can contribute to reducing costs and inflation problems that are currently facing.
It should be noted that the modernization of the banking system in the past, especially the payment system, contributed to the support of small and medium business activities during the Kovid 19 crisis and prevented the spread of disease by reducing the direct impact. Reduce unnecessary travel and maintain social gaps, while modernizing financial services has improved the financial environment or improved access to formal financial services in rural areas.
She added that, of course, digitalization has enabled people in remote areas to access much-needed services, including financial services, which they would not have been able to access without technological modernization, but digitalization also required factors. Also fundamental is the availability of affordable electronics and internet services, otherwise digitalization could lead to increased social inequality.
2. Skills and human resource development: The development of affordable digital infrastructure is not enough to increase the use of digital systems if digital literacy among the population is low. In addition, people with low digital skills are often at high risk of being easily deceived online, which leads to losses.
In this regard, Cambodia needs to make more efforts to integrate digital knowledge by ensuring that all people of all ages are equally developed and that no individual is abandoned in the digital age, combined with the technical skills of the people. Citizens of all ages to achieve a vision of fruitful digital economic and social development.
Therefore, existing and non-digital services that have been introduced by private and public institutions in the past should continue to be used in conjunction with digital services to ensure that all citizens, especially the elderly. Older people still have easy access to services.
3. Social protection: The Kovid-19 crisis has affected people of varying magnitude, with the poorest and most vulnerable affected by the current inflation crisis. The poorest people are also the poorest.
In Cambodia, the food and energy sector accounts for about 60% of the total consumer price index, so rising food and energy prices have left poor and vulnerable families with little or no savings. In order to maintain the power of purchasing daily necessities.
Therefore, the expansion of social protection is very important for poor and vulnerable families, while the promotion of companies, enterprises and employees in the informal economy into the informal economy is necessary to be able to access protection. Better yet.
In the past, the National Bank of Cambodia has also tried to alleviate the burden of the poor by stabilizing the exchange rate, which has contributed to reducing inflationary pressures. Recently, banking and financial institutions have been encouraged to restructure credit. For those affected by the floods.
In addition to the above three points, the development of small and medium enterprises, which is considered the backbone of Cambodia's economic development, by contributing to the creation of about 70% of total employment for the people of Cambodia and green economic development. Currently a topic of global debate, it should also be addressed, as Cambodia is an agricultural country vulnerable to climate risks.
