LONDON (Reuters) - Britain's Supreme Court ruled on Wednesday that Scotland could not hold a new independence referendum without the consent of the British government. The decision was a stumbling block to the Scottish Government's campaign to secede from Britain. This is according to the release by the AFP news agency on Wednesday, November 23, 2022.
The Supreme Court has ruled that the Scottish Parliament "has no power to pass legislation to hold a referendum on Scottish independence."
The Conservative British government in London refused to approve the election, saying the issue was resolved in a 2014 referendum that saw Scottish voters reject independence by a margin of 55% to 45%.
