Experts show record high watermelon nests in Mekong sanctuary in 4 years
(Kratie): With great excitement, the team of experts monitoring and researching the biodiversity of Sambo Wildlife Sanctuary reported today that they recorded the presence of 48 small watermelon nests, also confirming the presence of A total of 68 adult birds, as well as many young, are living in the floodplains of the Mekong River.
This encouraging result is part of a broader effort to research and monitor birds during the spawning season of this large bird from October to December. By rangers from the Kratie Provincial Department of Environment of the Ministry of Environment and WWF wildlife experts, as well as members of local communities living near the sanctuary.
WWF biodiversity experts describe the current results as a record high number of watermelon nests for the last four years in the Mekong sanctuary. In addition, the number of nests recorded now has increased by almost 130% compared to only 24 nests recorded by experts during the same period in 2021.
The Secretary of State also called for an end to traps and pollution that have serious consequences for wildlife survival.
A spokesman for the Ministry of Environment added: "I would like to urge all those who like to eat wild animals to stop eating and stop buying wild animals to jointly conserve all Cambodian wildlife."
At the same time, Mr. Neth Pheaktra also noted: "The increase in the presence and number of some wildlife is a sign of improved safety in the protected area of Cambodia, which is a result of management efforts. "And the protection of the Ministry of Environment, all partner organizations and stakeholders to provide safe shelter, rich in food and enough water for the survival of all species."
The Ministry of Environment and WWF, funded by the Government of the Kingdom of Belgium and WWF-Belgium, have been implementing bird-nest protection programs with the direct participation of local communities of the sanctuary in regular monitoring and patrol work led by Park officials to protect the eggs and nests of these otters until they hatch and fly out of business.
