The above program was coordinated by Dr. Va Sopheak, Director of the Office of Prevention and Control of Infectious Diseases of the Cambodian Red Cross and Secretary of the National Elite Working Group, leading the speakers to answer some questions focusing on the topic of The dialogue is "Joining the One Nation Toward the End of AIDS by 2025."
At the same time, the forum also listened to the intervention of Ms. Keo Sarady, an HIV patient in Svay Rieng province, who shared her real life experience of living with HIV for 27 years.
After paying close attention to the above forum and at the closing ceremony, Ms. Men Neary Sopheak, First Deputy Secretary General of the Cambodian Red Cross and Permanent Vice President of the National Elite Working Group, expressed her appreciation to the students. Who have studied and understood a wide range of issues related to HIV / AIDS.
In particular, the courage and wisdom to express their views with determination in the care of physical and mental health and in the context of HIV, as well as a strong commitment to participate in a single national trend towards the end of AIDS. In 2025.
At the same time, students have a constructive request to promote HIV / AIDS for equal access and dignity in life on an equal footing with the general population and to receive quality medical services. She also thanked Ms. Keo Sarady for sharing her real-life experience as a person living with HIV.
On the Day of Optimism for Equality Towards the End of AIDS in 2025, she brought the message of call from His Excellency Dr. Bun Rany Hun Sen, President of the Cambodian Red Cross and National Outstanding Leadership Forum. Asia-Pacific Response to HIV / AIDS and Development in Cambodia
1: Let all compatriots, the private sector, national and international organizations join forces in the same national current, prevent infectious diseases to end HIV / AIDS by 2025 with equality in society.
2: Compatriots, especially young men and women, must constantly find out new knowledge on the prevention of HIV transmission and the spread of HIV to protect ourselves, our families, our community and our society.
4: Pregnant women should seek care and check-ups at least 4 times and regularly according to the instructions of medical staff for maternal and child health, especially to help prevent the transmission of HIV and syphilis from mother to child. Baby
6: High-risk members, including men who have sex with men, transgender people and women who work in adult entertainment institutions, should continue to pay attention to the prevention of HIV and other infectious diseases, both now and in the future. .
Leaders of the working group, representatives from both universities, teachers, speakers, students all took photos and received certificates of appreciation, letters of thanks and souvenirs from Samdech Kittiprittbandit Bun Rany Hun Sen, national dignitaries. Everyone.
