TOKYO (Reuters) - Japan's economy shrank unexpectedly in the third quarter as a weaker yen weakened, boosting growth momentum. Outbreaks appear to be exacerbated. According to Bloomberg News.
Gross domestic product fell 1.2 percent year-on-year in the third quarter as a weaker yen boosted spending, the Bloomberg news agency reported, citing figures from the Japanese Cabinet Office on Tuesday. On Japanese imports. Economists expect the economy to grow 1.2 percent.
The dramatic slowdown reflects the impact of a devaluation of Japan's currency on the economy and shows that the road to a strong recovery from an epidemic remains a long way off. There are more risks covering the economic outlook.
Chinese real estate investment falls sharply in first 10 months of this year
BEIJING: China's real estate investment fell sharply in the January-October period, falling 8.8 percent from a year earlier after falling 8 percent. In the first nine months of this year. According to Reuters.
Reuters also reported that sales of real estate measured in square meters fell 22.3 percent in the same period last year, according to data released by the National Bureau of Statistics (NBS). Last year, compared with a 22.2 percent decline in the first nine months of this year.
New construction, measured in area, fell 37.8 percent in the first 10 months of this year from a year earlier, while in the first nine months of this year it fell 38 percent.
Funds raised by Chinese real estate developers fell 27.7 percent after falling 24.5 percent in the first nine months of the year. According to Reuters.
