(Banteay Meanchey): Mr. Oum Rath, Governor of Banteay Meanchey Province, requested rice traders as well as mill owners to help buy rice at a reasonable price to avoid lower prices or too low prices.
Farmers in Battambang and Banteay Meanchey provinces are rushing to harvest rice and have also erupted in complaints about falling rice prices.
On Wednesday, November 16, 2022, the Governor of Banteay Meanchey Province visited the rice fields that farmers are harvesting, meeting with farmers and finding solutions for them.
The governor of Banteay Meanchey province told Fresh News that during the visit to the farmers, he inspected the actual situation of the people's rice harvest and the price of rice, which has been declining for several weeks.
The provincial governor also confirmed that on November 17, he will have a meeting with the provincial Ministry of Agriculture, Commerce, traders and millers to discuss the price of rice farmers.
With the visit to check the price of rice of farmers, the governor also called on rice traders and millers to facilitate the purchase of rice farmers to avoid buying lower prices or lower prices. .
The provincial governor also instructed local authorities and stakeholders to check and coordinate with traders and farmers in the jurisdiction to find other ways to facilitate the purchase, collection and delivery.
Mr. Oum Rath said that in Banteay Meanchey province, the rice that people are harvesting is about 100,000 hectares. Collected about 20,000 hectares and the remaining 70 to 80,000 hectares.
It should also be noted that in connection with the fall in rice prices of farmers, the Ministry of Agriculture is also taking measures to help farmers.
Exporters expect China to become major pepper export market for Cambodia
(Phnom Penh): The Royal Government of Cambodia and the Government of China have already signed a Memorandum of Understanding on phytosanitary requirements for pepper exports from Cambodia to China.
The Memorandum of Understanding paves the way for the two countries' specialized ministries and institutions to continue negotiations on phytosanitary requirements to allow Cambodian pepper to be officially exported to China in the future.
The Cambodian Federation of Peppers and Spices, a group of pepper farmers and exporters, expects China to become an important new export market for Cambodia if the phytosanitary requirements are finalized and negotiations reached a formal export.
McNee, President of the Cambodian Pepper and Spices Federation, told Fresh News that if Cambodian pepper could be officially exported to China in the future, it would be of great benefit to Cambodia, especially pepper farmers and exporters. Get a new market in addition to the existing market.
He said that in the past, China did not seem to know much about Cambodian pepper, if Cambodian pepper could be exported to China, it would make the reputation of Cambodian pepper more famous in the Chinese market, which is a big market and boost exports. Mostly because the export of Cambodian pepper to China is done through Vietnam.
"If we can export directly to China, that would be very good for Cambodia, because China is a major importer of pepper, so we hope that Cambodia will be able to export more to China without relying on Vietnam," he said. "We will be able to export on our own through local exporters as well as Chinese investors who can buy directly from Cambodia rather than export through other countries."
However, Nguon Lay, president of the Kampot Pepper Promotion Association, a GI registered pepper, did not have high expectations in the Chinese market due to the high price of Kampot pepper, which is not in high demand in China. He said that in the past, Chinese traders have come to study the export of Kampot pepper to the Chinese market, but not the same price.
The pepper market in 2022 is not very good due to the global economic crisis, which led to a decline in pepper exports and caused the price of pepper to fall to 10,000 riel per kilogram, unlike in the past, farmers can sell at a round price. 14,000 to 15,000 riel per kilogram, which makes pepper farmers lose money this year, while the cost of fertilizers, pesticides, electricity and labor costs.
