Cambodia, India Sign Memorandum of Understanding on Cooperation in Biodiversity Conservation, Sustainable Wildlife Management and Tiger and Wildlife Restoration Strategies (Video inside) (Phnom Penh): Cambodia and India Sign a Memorandum of Understanding on Cooperation Collaborate on biodiversity conservation, sustainable wildlife management, and strategies for restoring large tigers and their habitats in Cambodia.
In terms of peace and political stability, economic growth, and the socio-economic and cultural changes of the Cambodian people, Cambodia has the opportunity to bring back rare species in Cambodia, especially the big tiger.
It should be noted that Cambodia participated in the International Forum in 2010 on the conservation and doubling of the number of big tigers in the forest by 2022 and prepared its action plan on big tigers (2015-2024). The Royal Government of Cambodia supports the Big Tiger Rehabilitation Program, and political support for this program has made Cambodia the first country in ASEAN to bring big tigers back into the wild.
The signing of this Memorandum of Understanding is a new page in the history of enhancing relations and friendship and cooperation between Cambodia and India, especially between the Ministry of Environment of the Kingdom of Cambodia and the Ministry of Environment, Forestry and Climate Change of the Republic of India in the field. Environment and conservation of natural resources.
This Memorandum of Understanding will benefit both countries on the following issues:
A) Promote biodiversity conservation by focusing on the conservation and restoration of large tigers in Cambodia, especially in key landscape areas, including the Cardamom Mountains and the eastern highlands, to prevent them from becoming extinct in Cambodia.
B) Provide breeding of big tigers from India and promote cooperation to prevent big tigers from living in the natural forest successfully. Share and exchange skills and abilities to promote tiger conservation in both countries.
C) Promote wildlife conservation by sharing good experiences of implementing technology, livelihoods for communities living in wildlife sanctuaries, and sustainable biodiversity management. The two countries will support progress in the framework of this cooperation and respect each other's views.
