Myanmar has been embroiled in bloody riots and violence since the Myanmar military led by Min Aung Lan staged a coup to oust Aung San Suu Kyi's civilian government in February 2021.
The UN chief made the statement in front of the media after a meeting with ASEAN leaders in Phnom Penh, Cambodia.
"The situation in Myanmar is like a never-ending nightmare for the people and a threat to peace and security in the region as a whole," UN chief Guterres said.
"I urge the Myanmar authorities to listen to their people, release political prisoners and restore democracy immediately, as this is the only way for stability and peace," he added.
Speaking at a press conference in Phnom Penh after co-chairing the ASEAN-UN Summit yesterday, the UN Secretary-General expressed his desire to see Myanmar's military regime fully implement the five-point agreement. Launched by ASEAN. He expects Indonesia, which will take over the rotating chairmanship of ASEAN in 2023, to work to resolve the issue in Myanmar as well.
According to Antonio Guterres, systematic human rights abuses in Myanmar are unacceptable and cause great pain to the people of Myanmar. He blamed the international community for failing to resolve the crisis in the country.
During the 40th and 41st ASEAN Summits on 11 November 2022, all ASEAN leaders evaluated and issued their resolutions on the implementation of the five points of the Myanmar Consensus Agreement. ASEAN leaders reaffirmed that Myanmar remains an important part of ASEAN and considered Articles 7 and 20 of the ASEAN Charter and recalled the 38th and 39th ASEAN Summits.
Following this assessment, the ASEAN Leaders at the Summit in Cambodia made the following decisions:
* The five-point unanimous agreement must remain our valid reference and should be fully implemented.
* There needs to be an implementation plan that shows concrete, tangible and measurable indicators with specific timelines to support a five-point consensus and must be developed. Assign the ASEAN Foreign Ministers to prepare this implementation plan.
* Strive for all stakeholders to adhere to and implement the five-point unanimous agreement and for ASEAN to contact all stakeholders as soon as possible. Communication will be conducted in a flexible and informal manner, especially by the Special Envoy of the ASEAN Chair on Myanmar, due to the neutrality associated with his mandate, which has the sole purpose of restoring peace and stability in the country in accordance with the agreement. Five points unanimous.
While maintaining Myanmar's non-political representation at the ASEAN Summit and the ASEAN Foreign Ministers' Meeting, we have entrusted the ASEAN Coordinating Council with a further review of Myanmar's representation in the ASEAN Summit. ASEAN Meeting if the situation requires.
* We reaffirm that the ASEAN Summit is the highest decision-making body and will make the final decision on the implementation of the five-point unanimous agreement, including when a consensus cannot be reached in accordance with the ASEAN Charter.
* Urges all parties concerned to ease tensions and tolerance as much as possible, and all armed stakeholders should be transparently responsible and condemn the violence, noting that the Myanmar Armed Forces is a military force. The largest single in Myanmar.
* ASEAN will call on the United Nations and our external partners to support us in our efforts to implement the five-point unanimous agreement.
* ASEAN must consider other ways to support the implementation of the five-point unanimous agreement.
We need to review the above decision at the next session and assign the Foreign Minister to monitor the progress and report to the ASEAN Summit.
