Chinese Premier Li Keqiang and his wife and a delegation arrived in Cambodia on Tuesday night for an official visit from November 8 to 11, 2022. In addition to his official visit, Li Keqiang will continue to attend ASEAN-related summits.
Starting his second official visit to Cambodia on the afternoon of November 9, before meeting with Samdech Techo Prime Minister, Mr. Li Keqiang laid a wreath and paid his respects at the Independence Monument, then laid a wreath and paid his respects. The body of His Majesty the King at the statue.
On November 10, Mr. Ly Ker Chheang and his wife will visit Siem Reap to visit some temples and will also preside over the signing ceremony of the reconstruction project of Takeo Temple.
On November 11, Mr. Ly Ker Chheang will pay homage to the King of Cambodia, His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni.
Mr. Li Keqiang graduated with a degree in Economics, Law and Economics from the Institute of Economics, Peking University, and holds a Master's Degree, a Bachelor's Degree in Law and a Doctor of Economics.
Li Keqiang was elected the seventh Prime Minister of China by the Chinese Communist Party after Wen Jiabao in 2013. He will step down as Chinese Prime Minister in March 2023.
In 2018, Mr. Ly Ker Chheang paid an official visit to Cambodia once.
Please note that Cambodia and China have been connected since July 19, 1958 for about 64 years. On December 13, 2010, the two countries' relations were upgraded to a comprehensive strategic partnership, a high-level relationship.
Through this top-level relationship, Cambodia and China have established many cooperation agreements on political, economic and people-to-people contacts. Through this relationship, the exchange of royal visits and frequent visits between the two countries was made.
His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni has visited China 21 times. Samdech Techo has been to China 14 times.
Apart from the previous leaders, the current Chinese leaders are all visiting Cambodia. Xi Jinping pays state visit in 2016, Li Keqiang pays official visit in 2018 During the visit, 19 documents were signed. They include economics, technological cooperation, infrastructure construction, agriculture, energy and health.
