(Kampong Thom): The Moon Festival and Ok Ambok are celebrated by the Cambodian people every year and never missed, except during the Khmer Rouge regime.
This ceremony is usually held during the low water season in the lunar month or November, which is the full moon day, which coincides with the 15th day of the lunar month, associated with the Royal Water Festival. The Moon Festival and Ok Ambok are traditional Khmer traditions that are inherited from ancient times long ago.
In fact, on the night of November 8, 2022, in Tang Kork district, a ceremony was held to celebrate the moon with the participation of many people, including the Tang Kork district administration led by Mr. Hak Mong Huot, the district governor. Led the three forces, civil servants under the district administration to participate in the ceremony at Wat Kuhonkor, located in Pong Ror commune, Tang Kork district, Kampong Thom province.
During the ceremony, there was also a rice threshing ceremony, which showcased the culture and activities of the farmers in the past.
Speaking at the event, Mr. Hak Mong Huot, the district governor, also thanked the pagoda's chief priest, who always started the festival every year to create an atmosphere for the next generation to understand and entertain Buddhists.
He also said that because our country is peaceful, we prevent the spread of Kovid, so all pagodas can hold this ceremony.
Please also note that the people who participated in the ceremony at each pagoda in Tang Kork district, more than 30,000 people.
