November 9 This year marks the 69th anniversary of Cambodia's independence from the French colony.
(Phnom Penh): November 9, 2022 is the 69th anniversary of the complete liberation of the entire Cambodian nation from the French colony under the ferocious crusade of the King of Cambodia, His Majesty the King. Samdech Norodom Sihanouk.
The celebration of the 9th November Independence Day In addition to Phnom Penh, the provinces also held celebrations to mark the historic day of the recapture of the rights and freedoms of the Cambodian people. Also.
In Asia, France focused on southern China and countries in Indochina such as Cambodia, Vietnam, Laos, and Siam. In these lands, Catholic priests have long been secretly involved in the preaching work through the people of Jesus.
France saw great benefits after coming to be a Khmer neighbor on Cochinchina (Kampuchea Krom). First, France saw the Mekong River as an important waterway that could penetrate southern China. Second, they had to get rid of all the dangers that threatened their new colonies, especially those from Siam, with the British Empire behind them.
The French invasion of Cambodia at that time was not called by Cambodia like the reign of King Ang Duong to help protect the country from Siamese aggression. The French came to see the benefits of losing some of their colonies, remembering that Cambodia had invited them to be their guardians.
In 1949, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk demanded that France terminate the Guardianship Treaties signed in 1863 and 1884. In the same year, 1949, he signed the Treaty of Independence, in which France legally recognized the independence of the Kingdom of Cambodia.
From 1952 to 1953, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk went on a royal crusade to demand 100% independence for the motherland.
The following is a history of some of the Royal Crusades of the Khmer King to demand independence from the colonies on Cambodia for 90 years:
1: Under a treaty dated November 8, 1949, France legally recognized Cambodia's independence.
On March 5, 1953, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk sent a royal message to Vincent O'Reilly, President of the French Republic, emphasizing the need for France to expand its Khmer independence.
Attached to the royal message, this letter contains sufficient study, with arguments confirming the prince's demands and suggestions on the eventual transfer of power.
At Napul, March 18, 1953, then at Fontainebleau, April 3, 1953, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk wrote two more letters to Vincent Oriol and the French government. In addition to his views on Khmer independence and to request the French government to expedite this issue.
10: After realizing that all nations have an understanding of the demands for independence and the events in Cambodia, His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk returned to his homeland and settled in the autonomous region, which includes Siem Reap, Battambang. And Kampong Thom.
The military autonomy of these provinces was ceded to Cambodia, with a royal claim made in 1949 for Siem Reap, Kampong Thom, and in 1952 for Battambang.
His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk announced that he would return to the capital whenever France agreed to return to Cambodia the last treasures of independence.
11. Negotiations held in Phnom Penh under the guidance of the King and under the leadership of Mr. Pen Nuth were completely successful.
On August 29, 1953, an agreement was signed to transfer the power of the judiciary. On August 29, 1953, an agreement was signed to transfer police powers. At the end of October 17, 1953, an agreement on the military sovereignty of the Khmer people was signed throughout the Kingdom of Cambodia.
On November 9, 1953, eight months before the Geneva Conference, a ceremony was held in front of the Royal Palace dedicated to the withdrawal of French power from Cambodia.
The last black cloud that closed the Cambodian-French friendship completely disappeared after the full recognition of France in December 1954 on Cambodia's central sovereignty over the economy and finance.
The Cambodian delegation sent to Paris by His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk to request French recognition was led by Mr. Channak. After his death in Paris, the Cambodian delegation was led by Mr. Nhek Chulong and The last was led by Ochin, who was then deputy prime minister and accompanied by Son San.
On July 13, 1954, the signing of the Geneva Accords included the disputed rivals in Indochina.
At the Geneva conference, the King sent a delegation led by then-Foreign Minister Tep Phorn and Nhek Chulong, Kim Jong-un and Son San, who, under the direction of the King, learned that the conference had been withdrawn. Troops from a neighboring country leave the Kingdom of Cambodia.
Cambodia left the Geneva Conference "July-1954" without any damage at all. In contrast to Laos and Vietnam, the Kingdom of Cambodia was able to avoid territorial divisions.
14. His Majesty King Norodom Sihanouk must fulfill his contract with the people for at least 3 years. The Cambodian people gave him the title of "Father of National Independence" and the request of the deputies within the framework of the National Consultative Council of the Royal Government made him a "national hero" in accordance with His Majesty the King. His Majesty the King.
