6: Swedish PM seeks support from Turkey for joining NATO Swedish PM seeks support from Turkey for joining NATO
Sweden and Finland have abandoned their long-standing non-aligned policies and applied for membership in the NATO military alliance after Russian forces invaded Ukraine in February over fears that Russian President Vladimir Putin would step down. Putin could set the next target on their country.
But Turkey, which has joined NATO since 1952, has not yet given the green light to the two countries, accusing them of ignoring Ankara's security concerns. Erdogan's government is urging both countries to crack down on individuals it considers terrorists, including supporters of the Kurdish Workers' Party and those suspected of plotting a failed coup in Turkey in 2016.
Turkey has also called for the lifting of weapons sanctions imposed after its 2019 invasion of northern Syria to fight Kurdish militants. Sweden last month said it would lift the sanctions, a step seen as aimed at securing Ankara approval.
