Mr. Neth Pheaktra made this statement when he chaired a meeting to summarize the results of the Zero Trap Campaign on November 4, 2022 at the Ministry of Environment with the participation of the General Department of Protected Areas. Nature and representatives of national and international NGOs that are partners in organizing the Zero Trap Campaign.
At the same time, the Secretary of State of the Ministry of Environment thanked the partner organizations that participated in the Ministry of Environment to organize important campaigns to change people's attitudes towards eating wildlife and to promote the difficulties of traps on wildlife safety and People.
The Ministry of Environment, together with conservation and development partners, works together to provide livelihood options, including agricultural and ecotourism initiatives, to community members living in and around protected areas that traditionally rely on non-timber forest products and wildlife hunting. Tradition to take meat to eat and trade.
According to the results of the first six months of the Zero Trap Campaign in the six provinces, more than 3,000 people directly participated, more than 600 news articles were written and published on national and international media, and spread to more than 3 million people. .
In addition, 52 restaurants in 6 provinces targeted by the Trap Zero campaign have been declared non-selling venues made from wild animals, and these restaurants have received environmental commendation from the Ministry of Environment for their commitment and participation. Preventing illegal wildlife trade.
Mr. Net Pheaktra emphasized that the Zero Trap Campaign provided many benefits for the environment and natural resources, especially as part of efforts to provide wildlife sanctuaries by "preserving forests and wildlife." The general public is aware of the Zero Trap campaign and has received extensive information on what has been published from the Zero Trap campaign.
Both officials of the Ministry of Environment and civil society organizations working on conservation have expressed satisfaction with the results of the six-month zero-trapping campaign, emphasizing that it is the most urgent work that the Ministry of Environment and its affiliates are doing. Joint campaign to eradicate the trap that kills Cambodian wildlife every day.
It is not known how many traps are placed in Cambodia's protected areas each year, but according to data from the Ministry of Environment, more than 60,000 traps were removed in 2021. According to previous studies, only 20% of traps are removed from protected areas per year.
Mr. Net Pheaktra said that the Zero Trap campaign will not stop there, but will continue to be a rainstorm and will be extended to the Cardamom Mountains, such as Kampong Speu, Koh Kong, Pursat and other protected areas. There is poaching and illegal wildlife trade.
