Based on the history, Sino-Cambodian relations date back to the 13th century. In modern times, formal diplomatic relations between the two countries were established on July 19, 1958. Since then, as a neighbor and close partner, Cambodia and China have established and nurtured an inseparable "steel" friendship, going through many historical trials and tribulations.
In political terms, Cambodia is China's oldest and closest ally. The two countries exchange high-level state and official visits frequently and regularly. Cambodia has always been a staunch supporter of the one-China policy. Prime Minister Hun Sen has made it clear that "Cambodia will not change its one-China policy."
In economic terms, China is Cambodia's largest foreign investor, a major donor and an increasingly important trading partner of Cambodia. China's vision for global development and its closer relationship with Cambodia is seen through the record of Chinese investment in Cambodia.
In 2006, the two countries upgraded the bilateral relationship to "Comprehensive Cooperation Partnership" and in 2010 upgraded it to "Comprehensive Strategic Partnership".
In April 2019, Cambodia and China signed the "Action Plan 2019-2023 on Building a Sino-Cambodian Community with a Common Future." Under the action plan, the two countries are committed to implementing 31 measures in five areas: politics, security, economy, people-to-people contacts and multilateral cooperation.
More importantly, Cambodia's neutral and just stance on the South China Sea has contributed to the maintenance of peace, security and stability in the region, while promoting the mutual benefits resulting from the cooperation and cooperation. A comprehensive strategic partnership of mutual benefit between ASEAN and China.
