Malaysia raises interest rates again as inflation continues to rise
KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysia's central bank raised its benchmark interest rate at its fourth consecutive meeting on Thursday as it struggled to curb inflation amid a positive growth outlook. According to Reuters.
Including a rate hike on Thursday, the BNM raised its overall rate by 1 percent since May, from a record low of 1.75 percent.
Reuters quoted a statement issued by the Central Bank of Malaysia as saying that raising interest rates on Thursday would help "manage the risk of excessive demand on price pressures".
Inflation in Malaysia has been rising, with the consumer price index rising 4.5 percent in September from a year earlier but down slightly from 4.7 percent in August. The Malaysian government expects inflation to average 3.3 percent this year. According to Reuters.
