(Phnom Penh): Mr. Puy Kea, ASEAN Expert Journalist and Head of the Office of the Japanese News Agency Kyodo Cambodia, shared some tips for journalists to cover the 40th and 41st ASEAN Summits and Activities. Relevant meeting of 2022, hosted by Cambodia from 10 to 13 November 2022.
Sharing the key points of this veteran journalist took place on the afternoon of November 4, 2022 in the program of the press conference on "Preparation for coverage of the ASEAN Summit and related summits". Organized by the Club of Cambodian Journalists (CJC). The event was also attended by representatives from the Ministry of Information and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and International Cooperation, as well as many journalists.
Puy Kea, an experienced journalist who has written more books on "ASEAN Secrets Behind the Scenes", outlines five key points that journalists should know and be prepared for before entering the news. The ASEAN Summit and related summits hosted by Cambodia will be attended by the world's top leaders and leaders of ASEAN member countries.
Five tips that start from Puy Kea's personal experience that are presented include:
* First: Know the schedule of the meeting, that is, know the specific time and general program from the first day until the end of the meeting to easily follow and capture important points from the meeting.
* Second: Know who is who? At this point, he explained that journalists need to know exactly who is attending the ASEAN Summit and the 34 related summits and their roles.
* Third: heat problem. At this point, he divided it into two parts, one for local journalists and the other for the international arena. Local coverage refers to what Cambodia benefits from this meeting. The international arena is divided into five main areas: Myanmar or Burma, the South China Sea, North Korea, China and Taiwan, and the Russia-Ukraine war.
* Fourth: Relevant documents in the meeting. He revealed that according to what he received, the main documents related to the meeting are up to 99 documents, which requires journalists to learn more about it.
* Fifth: A general survey of the organization of the private sector, including entertainment services, restaurants and other services to find out which countries or institutions have gone.
