So the launch of ballistic missiles on Thursday prompted Japan to warn its citizens to beware of ballistic missiles flying over the country. The Japanese government has warned northern and central Japanese citizens to seek refuge in their homes, but Tokyo has since made the adjustment, stating that the ballistic missile did not fly over Japan.
The Nov. 3 ballistic missile launch came after North Korea fired 23 test-fired missiles yesterday, the most in a single day. Yesterday, a bullet struck into South Korean waters near the South Korean island of Ulleung Island, but no one was injured.
